WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.100
A Bash script should invoke Git in a way that is easy to review and difficult to misuse.

2
00:00:06.200 --> 00:00:11.880
Avoid building one large command string from variable data and then asking the shell to interpret it again.

3
00:00:11.980 --> 00:00:14.940
Fixed command structure makes behavior clearer.

4
00:00:15.040 --> 00:00:21.520
Quoting repository URLs, refs, paths, and option values helps preserve literal boundaries.

5
00:00:21.620 --> 00:00:27.060
Bash arrays can be useful when a script needs to assemble options and operands while keeping them separate.

6
00:00:27.340 --> 00:00:30.300
Inputs to Git should be validated before use.

7
00:00:30.400 --> 00:00:42.160
Branch names, tag names, remote names, repository identifiers, directories, and release refs can all affect what the script checks out or what later automation trusts.

8
00:00:42.260 --> 00:00:48.180
A missing or unexpected ref should not silently fall back to a default that changes the release target.

9
00:00:48.280 --> 00:00:53.160
A repository URL should not be arbitrary when the workflow expects an approved source.

10
00:00:53.440 --> 00:00:58.880
Working directory control matters because Git behavior depends on which repository and checkout are active.

11
00:00:58.980 --> 00:01:09.140
A script that assumes it is inside one repository but runs from another can fetch, reset, describe, or package the wrong content.

12
00:01:09.240 --> 00:01:17.080
Use explicit workspace paths, verify repository identity where appropriate, and make directory transitions visible in the script.

13
00:01:17.180 --> 00:01:21.500
Avoid allowing leftover workspace state from one job to influence another.

14
00:01:21.780 --> 00:01:24.260
Result handling is part of safety.

15
00:01:24.360 --> 00:01:33.320
Scripts should check exit status, distinguish expected no-change states from failures, and stop before later steps trust a wrong checkout or incomplete operation.

16
00:01:33.420 --> 00:01:46.380
If a fetch fails, a checkout fails, a tag is missing, or a submodule update does not complete, the script should not continue to package, publish, or deploy as though the workspace is trustworthy.

17
00:01:46.480 --> 00:01:53.120
Reviewers can verify explicit workspace paths, repository identity, and visible directory transitions.
